<main>
  <p class="byline">Vyt Aitken · paper-cut studies · 12 October 2026</p>
  <h1>Field and<br>Channel</h1>
  <figure class="lead">
    <img src="overlap-underlip.png" alt="An angular terracotta cliff with open niches and a dark cut leading into a narrow opening above a blue channel.">
    <figcaption>Ninth cut.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>The land keeps more than one edge. This study stays abstract while I learn where the valley's different planning frames meet — and where they do not.</p>

  <section class="motion">
    <h2>The crossing in time</h2>
    <p><a href="the-joint.html">The Joint</a> lets the basin sheet slip beneath the cliff and briefly find the opening again. <a href="the-joint-held-beat.html">Held Beat</a> adds a short pause before the first drift. <a href="the-joint-close.html">Close Measure</a> brings the crossing forward.</p>
  </section>

  <details>
    <summary>Earlier cuts, trials, and source layers</summary>
    <div class="archive">
      <p>These studies show how the opening and crossing changed. Some are failures kept because they clarify what the later cut needed.</p>
      <figure><img src="overlap-held-interval.png" alt="A cliff and channel with an off-center notch and a split dark underlip."><figcaption>Tenth cut, Held Interval: the split underlip nearly disappears at small scale.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="overlap-offset-hold.png" alt="A cliff with a wider off-center opening above the blue channel."><figcaption>Eleventh cut, Offset Hold: the opening is clear, but begins to read as an arch.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="overlap-ledged-mouth.png" alt="The narrow cliff opening with a pale countermark moved closer to the crossing."><figcaption>Eighth cut: the pale mark moved closer, but still read as a separate strip.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="overlap-narrow-cut.png" alt="An angular terracotta cliff with a narrow opening above a blue channel and one pale countermark farther left."><figcaption>Seventh cut: a narrower aperture, with the countermark still detached.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="overlap-open-cut.png" alt="An angular terracotta cliff with open niches above a blue channel passing beneath a broad opening in the lower edge."><figcaption>Sixth cut: more air around the crossing, less force in the blue line.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="overlap-near-channel.png" alt="The same cliff and channel, with two pale marks; one crosses the water at the notch."><figcaption>Fifth cut: the countermark over the water made the crossing read like a bridge.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="overlap-counterledge.png" alt="An angular terracotta cliff with open niches above field bands and pale ledges at its lower edge."><figcaption>Third cut: a pale seam bridge, kept as a failed attempt at the crossing.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="edge-study.png" alt="A close cut of a terracotta cliff edge, notched where a blue channel bends through the field below."><figcaption>Edge study: the channel passes through an opening made by the cliff's own edge.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="overlap-ledged-profile.png" alt="A terracotta cliff with broad uneven ledges and open niches overlaps field bands; a blue channel passes through a small notch at its base."><figcaption>Earlier profile: softer ledges, less convincing as a cliff.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="overlap-study.png" alt="An ochre cliff with open niches meets bands of cultivated land and branching blue water. The cliff ends in a straight seam above the fields."><figcaption>First overlap: two sheets meet at a straight edge; the water does not yet cross clearly.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="overlap-seam-test.png" alt="The cliff and field layers, with a small notch where two blue branches meet."><figcaption>A notch gives the water a crossing, but the branching channel reads like a spring from the rock.</figcaption></figure>
      <figure><img src="overlap-channel-test.png" alt="A single blue channel runs beneath the cliff and appears at a small notch."><figcaption>One channel continues beneath the cliff and appears at the notch. Its course is invented, not a mapped waterway.</figcaption></figure>
      <section class="layers">
        <div><h2>Cliff sheet</h2><img src="cliff-sheet.png" alt="Ochre paper-cut cliff with two large niches and smaller cave openings."></div>
        <div><h2>Basin sheet</h2><img src="basin-sheet.png" alt="Sage and ochre field bands crossed by a pale road and one blue channel."></div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </details>

  <section class="other">
    <h2>Other pieces around the studio</h2>
